# i3 config file (v4)
#
# Please see https://i3wm.org/docs/userguide.html for a complete reference!

set $mod Mod4

# Colours come from the wallpaper. theme_init.sh regenerates colors.conf on
# every startup and every wallpaper change; it defines $base, $accent, $text
# and the client.* block, so it has to be included before anything uses them.
# (Generated file - it is not in the repo. Run theme_init.sh if it is missing.)
#
# The paths are absolute on purpose. This config is a stow symlink into the
# repo, and i3 resolves a *relative* include against the symlink's target
# directory - so `include colors.conf` looks inside dotfiles/.config/i3/,
# where the generated files never are. A missing include is not an error in
# i3, so that failure is completely silent.
include ~/.config/i3/colors.conf

# Which workspace lives on which monitor. monitors.sh regenerates this
# from whatever is plugged in, so the same config works docked and
# undocked. (Generated - run monitors.sh if it is missing.)
include ~/.config/i3/monitors.conf

# Font for window titles. Will also be used by the bar unless a different font
# is used in the bar {} block below.
font pango:Fantasque Sans Mono 12
# font pango:CaskaydiaCove Nerd Font 12

# This font is widely installed, provides lots of unicode glyphs, right-to-left
# text rendering and scalability on retina/hidpi displays (thanks to pango).
#font pango:DejaVu Sans Mono 8

# Arrange the monitors and regenerate monitors.conf. This runs before anything
# places a window, so workspaces land on the right screen from the first frame.
exec_always --no-startup-id $HOME/.config/i3/script/monitors.sh

# Start XDG autostart .desktop files using dex. See also
# https://wiki.archlinux.org/index.php/XDG_Autostart
# Spiral / fibonacci tiling.
#
# i3 has no such layout - its model is nested horizontal and vertical splits,
# and a new window always splits the focused container the same way, which is
# what makes everything stack down one axis. autotiling watches the focus and
# sets the split to whichever axis of the focused container is longer, so each
# new window halves the largest free space: wide container splits vertically,
# tall one splits horizontally. Repeat that and you get the spiral.
#
# exec_always, not exec: it has to come back after an i3 restart or the layout
# quietly reverts to splitting one way forever.
exec_always --no-startup-id autotiling

exec --no-startup-id dex --autostart --environment i3

# xss-lock grabs a logind suspend inhibit lock and locks the screen before
# suspend, and `loginctl lock-session` locks it on demand.
#
# It runs the themed locker, not bare i3lock: the palette and the wallpaper
# belong on the screen you actually see most of. --nofork is required - xss-lock
# tracks the locker as a child, and one that forks away looks like it exited,
# so the machine would suspend with the screen unlocked.
exec --no-startup-id xss-lock --transfer-sleep-lock -- $HOME/.config/i3/script/i3lock-color.sh --nofork

# What idling actually does, stated rather than inherited.
#
# X's built-in defaults are 600s for everything, which means the screen blanks
# and the panel powers off in the same instant: the lock screen you are about
# to need is drawn onto a display that is already dark, and if the panel then
# fails to wake you cannot tell a wedged machine from a sleeping one.
#
#   xset s 600 600      blank, and fire xss-lock -> the locker, at 10 minutes
#   xset dpms 0 0 900   panel off at 15 minutes; no separate standby/suspend
#                       stage, those do nothing useful on a laptop panel
#
# The five minutes between them is the point: the locker is up and visible
# before anything is powered down, so a black screen at 12 minutes means a
# real fault rather than normal behaviour.
#
# The panel not coming back from that is a separate, hardware-level bug - see
# /etc/modprobe.d/i915-psr.conf, which postinstall.sh writes.
exec_always --no-startup-id xset s 600 600 dpms 0 0 900

# NetworkManager is the most popular way to manage wireless networks on Linux,
# and nm-applet is a desktop environment-independent system tray GUI for it.
exec --no-startup-id nm-applet

# Automount external drives. udisks2 does the mounting, udiskie is what calls
# it - the rules are in .config/udiskie/config.yml.
#
# exec_always with a guard, not a bare exec. Both run at login; the
# difference is $mod+Shift+r, which re-runs exec_always and not exec. So exec
# alone means a udiskie that died stays dead until you log out, and a bare
# exec_always means a second one every restart, each racing the others to
# mount the same disk. The pgrep guard gives the useful half of both:
# restarting i3 revives it if it is gone and does nothing if it is not.
#
# (Reload - $mod+Shift+c - does not re-run exec_always at all here, despite
# what the name suggests: killing picom and reloading does not bring picom
# back either. Restart is the one that re-runs these.)
#
# The explicit `sh -c` is load-bearing and not a style choice. i3 runs an exec
# line as `sh -c "exec <line>"`, and that `exec` replaces the shell with the
# first command - so written bare, the shell *becomes* pgrep and the `|| ...`
# half is never reached. It fails silently: no error, no udiskie, and a config
# that looks right. Wrapping the logic in its own shell is what survives it.
exec_always --no-startup-id sh -c 'pgrep -x udiskie >/dev/null || udiskie'

# Volume, through script/volume.sh rather than pactl directly.
#
# The script exists for two things pactl on its own does not do: it clamps at
# 100% instead of letting `+10%` climb to a distorted 160, and it puts the
# level on screen as a progress bar. Holding a key redraws one popup rather
# than stacking one per press - see script/notify.sh.
#
# The `&& $refresh_i3status` these lines used to carry was signalling a program
# that is not running: the bar is polybar, whose alsa module follows the mixer
# by itself, and the bar {} block that would have started i3status is commented
# out at the bottom of this file.
bindsym XF86AudioRaiseVolume exec --no-startup-id $HOME/.config/i3/script/volume.sh up
bindsym XF86AudioLowerVolume exec --no-startup-id $HOME/.config/i3/script/volume.sh down
bindsym XF86AudioMute exec --no-startup-id $HOME/.config/i3/script/volume.sh mute
bindsym XF86AudioMicMute exec --no-startup-id $HOME/.config/i3/script/volume.sh micmute

# Use Mouse+$mod to drag floating windows to their wanted position
floating_modifier $mod

# move tiling windows via drag & drop by left-clicking into the title bar,
# or left-clicking anywhere into the window while holding the floating modifier.
tiling_drag modifier titlebar

# start a terminal
# bindsym $mod+Return exec i3-sensible-terminal
bindsym $mod+Return exec --no-startup-id kitty

# file manager
bindsym $mod+n exec --no-startup-id kitty -e ranger

# kill focused window
bindsym $mod+q kill

# re-detect monitors after plugging a screen in, then reload
# --notify: the screens go black for a moment and come back rearranged, and a
# short popup of what is now where is the only confirmation that it was this
# key that did it. The run at every i3 start deliberately has no --notify.
bindsym $mod+Shift+m exec --no-startup-id "$HOME/.config/i3/script/monitors.sh --notify && i3-msg reload && $HOME/.config/polybar/shades/launch.sh"

# move focus / the focused window between monitors
bindsym $mod+comma focus output left
bindsym $mod+period focus output right
bindsym $mod+Shift+comma move container to output left; focus output left
bindsym $mod+Shift+period move container to output right; focus output right

# The only wallpaper key: a new wallpaper, re-themed from it, on every screen.
# Where it comes from is $WALLPAPER_SOURCE (my_collection, bing or waifu),
# exported in ~/.xinitrc. theme_init.sh --bing / --waifu / --file still work
# from a terminal for a one-off from somewhere else.
bindsym $mod+Shift+w exec --no-startup-id $HOME/.config/i3/script/theme_init.sh --new

# start dmenu (a program launcher)
# bindsym $mod+d exec --no-startup-id dmenu_run
